.TH AXLISTEN 1 "29 September 2026" "pdn-ax25-tools" "Linux Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
axlisten \- show what is on an AX.25 channel
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B axlisten
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ port ...]
.br
.B axlisten \-\-classic
.RB [ \-8achirt ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B axlisten
prints every AX.25 frame a TNC hears, one header line per frame followed by
what the frame carries, decoded. Text is shown as text; APRS, NET/ROM
(including routing broadcasts and INP3), IP and ARP get a summary line; anything
else is hex dumped. APRS is decoded by Packet.Aprs and NET/ROM by Packet.NetRom,
the libraries the rest of the stack uses.
.PP
It only receives. It never transmits, needs no callsign, and is safe to run on
any port at any time.
.PP
Like
.BR axcall (1),
it speaks KISS to a TNC over a serial port or over TCP, and needs no kernel
AX.25 stack. The kernel version listened on a packet socket and so also heard
what the machine itself sent. A KISS TNC does not echo transmissions back to
the host, so what another program sends through the same TNC is not shown.
Everything on the air is.
.SH OUTPUT
The header line is the one
.B axcall \-d
prints, preceded by the time and the port:
.PP
.nf
.RS
18:45:51.123 radio  M0LTE\-7>GB7RDG SABM C P
18:45:51.520 radio  GB7RDG>M0LTE\-7 UA R F
18:45:51.521 radio  M0LTE\-7>GB7RDG I C P ns=0 nr=0 pid=F0 len=12
                    Hello there
18:45:52.004 radio  G4ABC\-1>APDW16 via WIDE1\-1*,WIDE2\-1 UI C pid=F0 len=46
                    APRS position 51.5000 \-0.1167, Car, 88\(de 36 kn [WB2OSZ DireWolf]
                    On the way to the rally
18:45:53.310 radio  GB7RDG>NODES UI C pid=CF len=48
                    NET/ROM nodes from RDG, 2 routes
                      CIP:GB7CIP        via GB7CIP    quality 200
                      GB7XYZ            via GB7CIP    quality 150
.RE
.fi
.PP
After the addresses come the frame type, then
.B C
or
.B R
for command or response, then
.B P
or
.B F
where the poll/final bit is set, then whatever that frame type carries.
A star after a digipeater means it has repeated the frame. A port that is one
channel of a multi-channel TNC is shown with its KISS port number, as in
.BR radio[1] .
Where the TNC reports signal strength, it is appended as
.B rssi=
and
.BR snr= .
.PP
An APRS summary names the device that sent it where the destination identifies
one, from the APRS device identification database. APRS units are the ones it
is sent in: knots, feet, Fahrenheit, miles per hour.
.PP
Control characters in received text are shown as dots and never reach the
terminal, since an escape sequence from anybody in range could otherwise
rewrite it.
.PP
Colour is used when the output is a terminal, unless
.B NO_COLOR
is set or
.B TERM
is
.BR dumb .
.SS Modulo 128
An I or S frame's control field is one octet on a modulo-8 link and two on a
modulo-128 link, and the frame does not say which. As the classic axlisten
did,
.B axlisten
reads a frame as modulo 128 when the first reserved bit of the source SSID is
clear, which is how the Linux kernel stack marked it. Stations that do not
mark it, Direwolf among them, have the I and S frames of their modulo-128
links misread.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I port
A port to listen on: a name from the ports file, a device path, or
.IR host : port
for a KISS-over-TCP modem. Give as many as you like; frames from all of them
are interleaved in the order they arrive. See
.BR axcall (1)
for the ports file. Only the transport column is used: the callsign, window
and paclen are for transmitting.
.IP
With no port, every port in the ports file is opened. One that cannot be
opened, because it is unplugged or another program holds it, is skipped with a
warning. Ports that name the same TNC are opened once.
.TP
.BI \-\-serial " device" [: baud ]
.TQ
.BI \-\-tcp " host" : port
Name a transport directly. Either may be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-\-baud " rate"
Serial baud rate for every serial port. Overrides a rate from the ports file.
The default is 57600.
.TP
.BI \-\-call " callsign"
Show only frames to, from or via this station. Without an SSID it matches
every SSID; with one, only that one. May be repeated, and a frame matching any
of them is shown.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-hex
Hex dump every payload, as well as decoding it.
.TP
.B \-\-json
One JSON object per frame, one per line, for a program to read. See JSON.
.TP
.BI \-\-color " when"
.BR auto ,
the default,
.BR always ,
or
.BR never .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
The same as
.BR "\-\-color never" .
.TP
.B \-\-utc
Timestamps in UTC, marked with a Z, rather than local time.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-time
No timestamps. Useful under
.BR journalctl (1),
which adds its own.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
No status messages on standard error. Frames and errors only.
.TP
.B \-\-classic
The output and options of the ax25\-apps
.BR axlisten .
See CLASSIC MODE.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Usage. With
.BR \-\-classic ,
.B \-h
is the hex dump, and only
.B \-\-help
is help.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Version, and the versions of the libraries it runs on.
.SH JSON
Each line is one object. Fields appear only where the frame carries them, so
.B ns
is only on I frames,
.B nr
only on I and S frames, and
.B pid
only where there is one.
.PP
.nf
.RS
{"time":"2026\-09\-14T18:45:51.123Z","port":"radio","kissPort":0,
 "from":"G4ABC\-1","to":"APRS","via":[{"call":"WIDE1\-1","repeated":true}],
 "type":"UI","cr":"command","pf":false,"pid":240,"len":5,
 "info":"SGVsbG8=","text":"Hello"}
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B info
is the information field in base64, always present when the field is not
empty.
.B text
is added when it reads as text, and
.B decoded
holds the summary lines of APRS, NET/ROM, IP or ARP that the text output shows.
.B cr
is
.BR command ,
.B response
or null for a version 1 frame that is neither.
.B mod128
is true on a frame read as modulo 128. A frame that cannot be decoded is
reported with
.B error
and its bytes as
.BR raw .
.SH CLASSIC MODE
.B \-\-classic
prints what the
.B axlisten
from ax25\-apps printed, for scripts, log parsers and habits formed on it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
radio: fm M0LTE\-7 to GB7RDG ctl SABM+ 18:45:51.123456
radio: fm GB7RDG to M0LTE\-7 ctl UA\- 18:45:51.520112
radio: fm M0LTE\-7 to GB7RDG ctl I00^ pid=F0(Text) len 5 18:45:51.521003
0000  hello
.RE
.fi
.PP
and takes its options, which cluster as they did:
.TP
.B \-a
Accepted and ignored. Every frame the TNC hears is shown anyway.
.TP
.B \-c
Colour, in ANSI escapes rather than through curses.
.TP
.B \-h
Hex dump the data, 16 bytes a line.
.TP
.B \-r
Readable dump: the data as lines of text.
.TP
.B \-8
Pass characters above 127. Without it they are shown as dots.
.TP
.B \-i
With
.BR \-8 ,
map IBM code page 437 characters 128 to 159.
.TP
.BI \-p " port"
Listen on this port. With none, every port in the ports file.
.TP
.B \-t
Repeat to choose the timestamp:
.B \-t
none,
.B \-tt
Unix time,
.B \-ttt
time since the previous frame,
.B \-tttt
date and time,
.B \-ttttt
time since the first frame. Without it, local time of day.
.PP
.BR \-\-serial ,
.BR \-\-tcp ,
.BR \-\-baud ,
.B \-\-call
and ports given as arguments work here too.
.PP
The differences are deliberate. SREJ, XID and TEST are named rather than
printed as
.BR [invalid] .
An I or UI frame with no PID, and a frame that ends after its addresses, still
end their line. The protocol decoders the original carried for ARP, IP,
NET/ROM, ROSE, FlexNet and OpenTRAC are not reproduced, so those payloads are
dumped like any other data; the default output decodes the ones still on the
air.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Stopped with Ctrl\-C, or the program reading the output went away.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, including naming no port when none are configured.
.TP
.B 3
A port named on the command line could not be opened, or none of the
configured ports could.
.PP
A port that goes away once open is not an exit. It is reopened, retrying with
a growing delay up to 30 seconds, for as long as
.B axlisten
runs, and each outage is reported on standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Everything, on every configured port:
.PP
.nf
.RS
axlisten
.RE
.fi
.PP
One station's traffic, on a Direwolf instance:
.PP
.nf
.RS
axlisten \-\-tcp localhost:8001 \-\-call GB7RDG
.RE
.fi
.PP
A log for a program:
.PP
.nf
.RS
axlisten \-q \-\-json radio >> /var/log/radio.jsonl
.RE
.fi
.PP
The old way, readable dump without timestamps:
.PP
.nf
.RS
axlisten \-\-classic \-art \-p radio
.RE
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AXCALL_PORTS
Read this ports file instead of the usual two.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set, no colour unless
.B \-\-color always
is given.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/axcall/ports
.TQ
.I ~/.config/axcall/ports
The ports file, shared with
.BR axcall (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR axcall (1),
.BR axsocks (1),
.BR axinetd (1),
.BR axtun (1)
.PP
